---
import { OFFICIAL_SOURCES } from '../../config/site';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const FAQ_ITEMS = [
  {
    question: 'When does a cracked heat exchanger require shutting off a furnace immediately?',
    answer:
      'Immediately. A cracked or rusted heat exchanger can leak carbon monoxide into indoor supply air. Shut down the furnace and contact a licensed HVAC technician or gas utility at once; safety hazards override any dollar rule.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Are capacitors, contactors, and flame sensors worth repairing on an older HVAC unit?',
    answer:
      'Usually yes. Those wear items cost a small fraction of a new system, require zero refrigerant work, and rarely justify condemning a healthy R-410A unit—especially when Checks B and D confirm repairing saves thousands.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why does an R-22 evaporator coil leak usually lean replace over age 12?',
    answer:
      'EPA banned virgin R-22 production on January 1, 2020, leaving scarce reclaimed refrigerant while the remaining 13+ year old outdoor compressor and coils stay out of warranty.',
  },
] as const;

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: FAQ_ITEMS.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.question,
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: item.answer,
    },
  })),
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Signs Your HVAC Is Dying vs. Worth One More Repair | HVAC Second Opinion"
  description="Tell immediate HVAC safety hazards and terminal compressor or coil failures apart from cheap wear-part repairs like capacitors, contactors, and sensors."
  structuredData={faqSchema}
>
  <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Calculator</a> / <a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / <span>Signs Your HVAC Is Dying</span>
  </nav>

  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Signs Your HVAC System Is Dying vs. Worth One More Repair</h1>
    <p class="intro-lead">
      A breakdown during peak summer or freezing winter does not automatically require a $10,000 system replacement. Many sudden shutdowns stem from $200–$500 electrical or sensor wear items, while a smaller group indicate safety risks or true end-of-life failures.
    </p>

    <h2>Group 1: Immediate Safety Issues (Never Delay)</h2>
    <p>
      Safety always overrides financial ratios in the <a href="/">calculator</a>. Shut the equipment down and call a licensed HVAC pro or gas utility immediately for:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cracked gas heat exchanger or CO alarm trip:</strong> Carbon monoxide leaks into supply air pose severe health hazards; outside heat-exchanger warranty coverage, replacement is normally required.</li>
      <li><strong>Tripped breakers, scorched wiring, or electrical burning odor:</strong> Shorted compressor windings or melted lugs indicate fire hazards and internal mechanical stress.</li>
      <li><strong>Active gas leaks or blocked exhaust flues:</strong> Combustible gas odors or corroded vents require immediate red-tag isolation.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Group 2: Terminal or High-Risk Failures (Strong Lean Replace Over Age 12)</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Out-of-warranty burned-out compressor:</strong> Swapping a failed compressor past age 10–12 costs thousands while leaving an old indoor coil, fan motor, and ductwork untouched.</li>
      <li><strong>Major R-22 refrigerant leak:</strong> Because <a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.epaR22} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. EPA banned virgin R-22</a> on January 1, 2020, costly coil leaks past age 12 trigger Check C (<a href="/guides/r22-refrigerant/">read our R-22 phase-out guide</a>).</li>
      <li><strong>Stacking major repairs plus efficiency losses:</strong> Multiple board/motor failures alongside rising bills mean cumulative repair spend approaches a <a href="/guides/seer2-explained/">DOE-compliant SEER2 upgrade</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <figure class="manual-figure">
      <img
        src="/images/fig-07-triage-diagnostic-tree.svg"
        alt="Diagnostic schematic separating Group 1 Safety Shutdowns, Group 2 Terminal Failures, and Group 3 Routine Wear Parts with worked examples."
        width="820"
        height="330"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Figure 07: Symptom triage matrix separating immediate safety shutdowns and terminal R-22/compressor failures from routine electrical wear parts.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Group 3: Routine Wear Parts (Usually Worth Repairing)</h2>
    <div tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Common HVAC wear parts vs terminal failures" class="table-wrapper">
      <table class="estimate-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Symptom / Part</th>
            <th>Why It Fails</th>
            <th>Typical Repair vs. Replace Lean</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Dual run capacitor</th>
            <td>Heat and voltage surges degrade the start/run capacitor.</td>
            <td>Almost always <strong>repair</strong> — minor wear part even past age 10.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Contactor or relay switch</th>
            <td>Pitted 240V contacts block compressor/fan power.</td>
            <td>Almost always <strong>repair</strong> — low-cost isolated switch.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Clogged drain / float switch</th>
            <td>Algae buildup trips safety float switch to stop water damage.</td>
            <td><strong>Repair / flush</strong> — zero compressor or coil wear.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Flame sensor or igniter</th>
            <td>Soot or brittle silicon nitride igniter stops furnace ignition.</td>
            <td><strong>Repair</strong> — standard periodic furnace service item.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Inducer or PSC blower motor</th>
            <td>Bearing wear after 8–12 years of daily heating/cooling cycles.</td>
            <td>Verify via <a href="/guides/5000-rule/">50% &amp; $5,000 rules</a> — usually <strong>repair</strong> on healthy R-410A rigs.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Worked Example: Reproducible Calculator Inputs for Two Same-Week Calls</h2>
    <div class="worked-example">
      <p>
        Enter these exact figures into the <a href="/">calculator</a> to verify both scenarios line by line:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Homeowner A (9-yr R-410A wear repair):</strong> Repair quote = <strong>$360</strong> (capacitor + contactor), Replacement quote = <strong>$9,500</strong>, Age = <strong>9</strong>, Refrigerant = <strong>R-410A</strong>, Stay = <strong>5 years</strong>, Expected future annual repairs = <strong>$150/yr</strong>, Expected energy savings = <strong>$100/yr</strong>. Check A = <code>9 × $360 = $3,240</code> (clear), Check B = <code>3.8%</code> (clear), Check C = clear, Check D = Repair total <code>$360 + (5 × $150) = $1,110</code> vs. Replace net <code>$9,500 − (5 × $100) = $9,000</code> — repairing saves <strong>$7,890</strong> (or <strong>$9,140</strong> with optional fields left at $0). Verdict: <strong>Lean Repair</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Homeowner B (15-yr R-22 coil failure):</strong> Repair quote = <strong>$3,200</strong> (coil + reclaimed R-22), Replacement quote = <strong>$10,400</strong>, Age = <strong>15</strong>, Refrigerant = <strong>R-22</strong>, Stay = <strong>5 years</strong>, Expected future annual repairs = <strong>$500/yr</strong>, Expected energy savings = <strong>$250/yr</strong>. Check A = <code>15 × $3,200 = $48,000</code> (fires), Check C = fired (age &gt; 12 + R-22), Check D = Repair total <code>$5,700</code> vs. Replace net <code>$9,150</code>. With an aging compressor already pulling high amperage, sinking $3,200 into obsolete R-22 triggers <strong>Lean Replace</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>How to Verify a “Condemned System” Claim on Site</h2>
    <p>
      High-pressure emergency calls on July weekends or sub-freezing nights create fertile ground for rushed replacements. Before agreeing to condemn an entire unit, ask the technician to document concrete measurements directly on your invoice or photo report:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Electrical readings for wear parts:</strong> For a failed capacitor or weak blower motor, ask to see the microfarad meter reading compared directly against the capacitor’s stamped specification (typically ±5%–10% tolerance) or motor winding amp draw against nameplate FLA.</li>
      <li><strong>Visual evidence for heat exchangers or leaks:</strong> For cracked gas heat exchangers, request dated borescope photos from your actual furnace rather than generic stock illustrations, or step outside to view electronic leak-detector confirmation on oily R-22 evaporator coils (<a href="/guides/r22-refrigerant/">review R-22 economics</a>).</li>
      <li><strong>Get a second diagnostic opinion before big spend:</strong> Paying a second licensed HVAC company a standard diagnostic service call is inexpensive insurance against replacing an entire $9,500–$10,400 system when the root problem is an isolated electrical switch or condensate drain fault.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Before Approving Emergency Work</h2>
    <p>
      Ask for a written diagnosis naming the exact failed component and its repair warranty before signing, review <a href="/guides/how-to-read-hvac-quote/">our complete quote checklist</a>, and check credentials using <a href="/guides/how-to-vet-hvac-contractor/">our contractor vetting guide</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-list">
      {FAQ_ITEMS.map((item) => (
        <details>
          <summary>{item.question}</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">{item.answer}</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
